  Casp1:
    'If you need genetics for deciding whether a cell survives or dies, start with Casp1. Pair a humanized Casp1 target with a human immune engraftment host if the readout is a biologic. For immuno oncology, humanized targets in the right immune context beat mouse only nulls. A global Casp1 knockout can scramble the whole immune system. Restrict the deletion to the cell type you care about. If systemic loss confounds the read, restrict the deletion. Do not force a global null for a cell type question.',
  Casp3:
    'Casp3 is the mouse ortholog of human CASP3. Think deciding whether a cell survives or dies. Native regulation knockins beat random overexpression for dosage sensitive cytokines like Casp3. KO, cKO, knockin, and humanized Casp3 alleles are what most immune programs actually need. Delete Casp3 in T cells, B cells, myeloid cells, or epithelium if you need to know which compartment drives the phenotype. Humanize for antibody work on CASP3. Use mouse nulls when you only need pathway necessity. If the assay is antibody blockade of CASP3, humanize Casp3 early. Use mouse nulls to show pathway need.',
