  Esr1:
    'Esr1 is the mouse form of ESR1. Most adult work here is really about estrogen receptor programs in breast, bone, or metabolism. Humanize Esr1 when your antibody or degrader prefers human ESR1 over mouse protein. Add a point mutant or humanized allele only when a clean null will not answer the question. If a related family member can compensate, plan the second conditional cross up front or the phenotype will look cleaner than it is. Most programs need some mix of null, floxed, knockin, and humanized Esr1 alleles as the question evolves.',
  Ezh2:
    'Tumor studies of Ezh2 start from driver signaling, DNA repair, or tumor niche biology. Humanize Ezh2 when the antibody, ADC, or degrader needs human EZH2 sequence in a mouse host. For oncogenes, activating knockins beat simple deletion of Ezh2. Most oncology programs escalate across null, floxed, knockin, and humanized Ezh2 alleles. If the biologic fails to cross react with mouse Ezh2, humanization is required, not optional. A conventional knockout works for tumor suppressor dosage if homozygous Ezh2 loss is tolerated long enough to score tumors.',
  F10:
    'For immune work on host defense, tolerance, and inflammatory signaling, F10 is the allele most people build. Single locus mutants often understate combination biology. Checkpoint or cytokine partner crosses are common with F10. If systemic loss confounds the read, restrict the deletion. Do not force a global null for a cell type question. Native regulation knockins beat random overexpression for dosage sensitive cytokines like F10. Pick the Cre that matches the compartment in your primary assay before you lock the allele.',
  F11:
    'For immune work on host defense, tolerance, and inflammatory signaling, F11 is the allele most people build. If the assay is antibody blockade of F11, humanize F11 early. Use mouse nulls to show pathway need. A global F11 knockout can scramble the whole immune system. Restrict the deletion to the cell type you care about. Pick the Cre that matches the compartment in your primary assay before you lock the allele. Native regulation knockins beat random overexpression for dosage sensitive cytokines like F11.',
  F12:
    'F12 (F12) comes up constantly in infection, autoimmunity, and tumor challenge. Reporter alleles let you track F12 positive cells during challenge without permanently removing the pathway. Pair a humanized F12 target with a human immune engraftment host if the readout is a biologic. For immuno oncology, humanized targets in the right immune context beat mouse only nulls. If the assay is antibody blockade of F12, humanize F12 early. Use mouse nulls to show pathway need. A global F12 knockout can scramble the whole immune system. Restrict the deletion to the cell type you care about.',
  F13a1:
    'F13a1 is the mouse ortholog of human F13A1. Think host defense, tolerance, and inflammatory signaling. Native regulation knockins beat random overexpression for dosage sensitive cytokines like F13a1. KO, cKO, knockin, and humanized F13a1 alleles are what most immune programs actually need. Delete F13a1 in T cells, B cells, myeloid cells, or epithelium if you need to know which compartment drives the phenotype. Humanize for antibody work on F13A1. Use mouse nulls when you only need pathway necessity. If the assay is antibody blockade of F13A1, humanize F13a1 early. Use mouse nulls to show pathway need.',
