  F9:
    'If you need genetics for host defense, tolerance, and inflammatory signaling, start with F9. Delete F9 in T cells, B cells, myeloid cells, or epithelium if you need to know which compartment drives the phenotype. For immuno oncology, humanized targets in the right immune context beat mouse only nulls. Humanize F9 when clinical antibodies see human F9 epitopes that mouse protein lacks. If systemic loss confounds the read, restrict the deletion. Do not force a global null for a cell type question.',
  Fahn:
    'Circuit and disease studies of Fahn are really about circuit function or neurodegeneration. Region restricted deletion avoids developmental wiring defects that hide adult neurodegeneration phenotypes at Fahn. Wiring, acute injury, and chronic proteinopathy need different KO versus knockin choices. If the binder only recognizes human FAHN, humanize Fahn before you invest in a large behavior study. Most neuroscience programs need some mix of null, floxed, knockin, and humanized Fahn alleles. Antibody and ASO programs need human sequence at Fahn. Mouse only genetics leave a gap.',
