  Grn:
    'If the question is circuit function or neurodegeneration, Grn is the locus I would build. If you need aged cohorts, decide early whether you want a strong null or a milder hypomorphic knockin at Grn. For adult brain questions, cKO or disease knockins beat harsh developmental nulls. Reporter alleles map Grn across neuron and glia subtypes without removing function. Wiring, acute injury, and chronic proteinopathy need different KO versus knockin choices. If the binder only recognizes human GRN, humanize Grn before you invest in a large behavior study.',
  Gsdmd:
    'Gsdmd is the mouse ortholog of human GSDMD. Think host defense, tolerance, and inflammatory signaling. Inducible Cre after immune education finishes separates developmental effects from adult effector function. Human sequence at Gsdmd also helps when your diagnostic reagent only recognizes the human protein. Humanize for antibody work on GSDMD. Use mouse nulls when you only need pathway necessity. Delete Gsdmd in T cells, B cells, myeloid cells, or epithelium if you need to know which compartment drives the phenotype. For immuno oncology, humanized targets in the right immune context beat mouse only nulls.',
  Gsk3b:
    'Gsk3b (GSK3B) is the locus I would reach for when the experiment needs Wnt pathway control of growth and patterning. Adult deletion of Gsk3b is usually cleaner than taking it out from birth. Humanize Gsk3b when your antibody or degrader prefers human GSK3B over mouse protein. Add a point mutant or humanized allele only when a clean null will not answer the question. Point mutant knockins make sense when human GSK3B variants change activity instead of removing the protein. Most programs need some mix of null, floxed, knockin, and humanized Gsk3b alleles as the question evolves.',
  H3f3a:
    'H3f3a is the mouse ortholog of human H3F3A. Oncology work here is about driver signaling, DNA repair, or tumor niche biology. Inducible timing lets you treat established disease instead of nascent lesions. If the null is embryonic lethal, go conditional or mosaic. Do not force a homozygous knockout for an adult tumor study. Patient variant knockins get you into pharmacology that matches clinical H3F3A mutations. A conventional knockout works for tumor suppressor dosage if homozygous H3f3a loss is tolerated long enough to score tumors.',
  H3f3b:
    'H3f3b (H3F3B) shows up on therapy and resistance paths that need defined cohorts. If the null is embryonic lethal, go conditional or mosaic. Do not force a homozygous knockout for an adult tumor study. If early lethality kills the adult tumor window, start with conditional H3f3b. Orthotopic designs with engineered H3f3b alleles beat simple overexpression grafts for niche questions. Decide the readout first. Tumor control, metastasis, and drug response point to different alleles. Humanize H3f3b when the antibody, ADC, or degrader needs human H3F3B sequence in a mouse host.',
  Havcr2:
    'Human HAVCR2 maps to mouse Havcr2. That is the locus for host defense, tolerance, and inflammatory signaling. Some papers also call it TIM 3. If systemic loss confounds the read, restrict the deletion. Do not force a global null for a cell type question. Inducible Cre after immune education finishes separates developmental effects from adult effector function. Human sequence at Havcr2 also helps when your diagnostic reagent only recognizes the human protein. Reporter alleles let you track Havcr2 positive cells during challenge without permanently removing the pathway.',
  Hes1:
    'Hes1 is Notch mediated fate decisions. That is the mouse form of HES1. If the compound only binds human HES1, humanize first and use a mouse null for mechanism. Floxed Hes1 plus a Cre you trust is enough for most adult signaling readouts. Adult deletion of Hes1 is usually cleaner than taking it out from birth. Cre specificity matters as much as the flox design. Validate the driver before you scale. Humanize Hes1 when your antibody or degrader prefers human HES1 over mouse protein.',
  Hey1:
    'Hey1 (HEY1) is the locus I would reach for when the experiment needs Notch mediated fate decisions. A Hey1 allele crossed to a pathway partner often beats studying the gene alone. A constitutive null is fine if the animals live. If they do not, flox Hey1 and delete with tissue or inducible Cre. Add a point mutant or humanized allele only when a clean null will not answer the question. Reporter knockins tell you where Hey1 is expressed without removing every transcript.',
  Hif1a:
    'Hif1a is hypoxia driven transcriptional rewiring. That is the mouse form of HIF1A. If the compound only binds human HIF1A, humanize first and use a mouse null for mechanism. Floxed Hif1a plus a Cre you trust is enough for most adult signaling readouts. Adult deletion of Hif1a is usually cleaner than taking it out from birth. Cre specificity matters as much as the flox design. Validate the driver before you scale. Humanize Hif1a when your antibody or degrader prefers human HIF1A over mouse protein.',
  Hist1h3b:
    'Tumor studies of Hist1h3b start from driver signaling, DNA repair, or tumor niche biology. Tissue restricted or inducible alleles let tumors arise after development, closer to how patients acquire HIST1H3B lesions. Inducible timing lets you treat established disease instead of nascent lesions. If the biologic fails to cross react with mouse Hist1h3b, humanization is required, not optional. Most oncology programs escalate across null, floxed, knockin, and humanized Hist1h3b alleles. Patient variant knockins get you into pharmacology that matches clinical HIST1H3B mutations.',
  Hist1h3c:
    'If the genetics story is driver signaling, DNA repair, or tumor niche biology, Hist1h3c is the allele I would build. Decide the readout first. Tumor control, metastasis, and drug response point to different alleles. Tissue restricted or inducible alleles let tumors arise after development, closer to how patients acquire HIST1H3C lesions. Add humanized checkpoint partners when the readout is immunotherapy rather than tumor genetics alone. For oncogenes, activating knockins beat simple deletion of Hist1h3c. Most oncology programs escalate across null, floxed, knockin, and humanized Hist1h3c alleles.',
  Hist1h3d:
    'Hist1h3d is the mouse ortholog of human HIST1H3D. Oncology work here is about driver signaling, DNA repair, or tumor niche biology. For oncogenes, activating knockins beat simple deletion of Hist1h3d. Most oncology programs escalate across null, floxed, knockin, and humanized Hist1h3d alleles. Patient variant knockins get you into pharmacology that matches clinical HIST1H3D mutations. If early lethality kills the adult tumor window, start with conditional Hist1h3d. Orthotopic designs with engineered Hist1h3d alleles beat simple overexpression grafts for niche questions. Tissue restricted or inducible alleles let tumors arise after development, closer to how patients acquire HIST1H3D lesions.',
  Hist1h3e:
    'If the genetics story is driver signaling, DNA repair, or tumor niche biology, Hist1h3e is the allele I would build. Orthotopic designs with engineered Hist1h3e alleles beat simple overexpression grafts for niche questions. Suppressors: flox or null. Drivers: activating knockin. Biologics aimed at HIST1H3E: humanize. For oncogenes, activating knockins beat simple deletion of Hist1h3e. Inducible timing lets you treat established disease instead of nascent lesions. If the biologic fails to cross react with mouse Hist1h3e, humanization is required, not optional.',
